   "subject": "Re: Set the Estimate and Std Err of path coefficients",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">     Yes, the standard error is a by-product You could manipulate conditions to induce the standard error to be larger or smaller. You have sample size as a lever to pull, of course, but if you want to manipulate sample size separately, then your main levers are collinearity and R2 for the dependent variable. For regression-type coefficients (which includes factor loadings), recall that the base formula for those reduces to covariance (y,x) / variance (x), so the variance of the dependent variable is somewhat separate. You can spike the dependent variable with random variance to reduce R2, for example. As R2 goes up, standard error goes down. You could manipulate collinearity if there are multiple predictors. Higher collinearity would mean larger standard errors. That would also have an effect on R2, so you would need to be meticulous. But you could do it, I think.<div>     In some cases, identificatiuon could be used to manipulate standard error. A model with marginal identification will have larger standard errors. But if you are doing simulations and working at the edges of identification, then some fo your replications will likely fail identification entirely and fail to converge.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Thu, Apr 11, 2019 at 9:53 AM Rönkkö, Mikko &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mikko....@jyu.fi</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div>\r\nHi,\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>No, you cannot set the estimate or the standard error. </div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>What you can do and indeed do below is to set the population values. The estimates converge to those population values if the estimator is consistent and the sample size is large. So you are not really setting the estimates, but your scenario\r\n is such that the estimates will get close to the population values. </div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>There is no population counterpart for a standard errors, which simply estimates the precision of the estimates.</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Mikko</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n<div><br>\r\n<blockquote type=\"cite\">\r\n<div>On 11 Apr 2019, at 10.32, MARIO GARRIDO ESCUDERO &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">gaia...@usal.es</a>&gt; wrote:</div>\r\n<br>\r\n<div>\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\"><font face=\"verdana, sans-serif\">Hi users,</font>\r\n<div><font face=\"verdana, sans-serif\">I made a similar question previously but I think it was my error to not express it well. </font></div>\r\n<div><font face=\"verdana, sans-serif\">The question is, when generating data, can I set the Estimate and Std Err of path coefficients (the ones in bold)?</font></div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;color:rgb(80,0,80);line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\">\r\n<span style=\"font-family:Garamond,serif;color:rgb(0,112,192)\">Regressions:<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p cla